  "Number": 6,
  "Question": "The sum of two positive numbers is \\(5\\) times their difference. What is the ratio of the larger number to the smaller number?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{5}{4}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{9}{5}\\)",
    "D": "\\(2\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{5}{2}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "The sum is positive, so its fifth, the difference, is also positive. Use that difference as one unit. The larger number then exceeds the smaller by exactly \\(1\\) unit, and their sum is \\(5\\) units.\n\nSet aside the larger number's extra unit. The two remaining amounts are equal and total \\(5-1=4\\) units, so each is \\(2\\) units. Thus the original smaller number is \\(2\\) units and the larger is \\(3\\) units. Their ratio is\n\\[\n\\frac{\\text{larger}}{\\text{smaller}}=\\boxed{\\frac32},\n\\]\nchoice B.",
